<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Miss Anthony is an 85-year-old woman with heart failure with preserved ejection fraction (ejection fraction 62 percent) and hypertensive heart disease, euvolemic since June on as-needed furosemide, who asked for a same-day visit. Four days ago, two days after returning by train from a convention, she developed a runny nose and sore throat, followed by a dry cough that is worse at night and has woken her on two nights. Her sister had a cold the week before. A home COVID-19 antigen test on day two was negative. Her highest home temperature was 37.6 °C. Her daily weights have stayed between 56.0 and 56.4 kg, so she has not needed an extra furosemide dose. She is mildly breathless only during coughing fits, still sleeps on one pillow, and has kept walking, more slowly. Honey in hot tea eases the cough. She has taken nothing else, because the care plan told her to avoid cold remedies containing decongestants or ibuprofen. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Four days ago, two days after train travel</td></tr><tr><td>Location</td><td>Nose, throat, and chest</td></tr><tr><td>Duration</td><td>Four days</td></tr><tr><td>Character</td><td>Dry cough; clear nasal discharge; scratchy throat</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>Mild to moderate; sleep broken on two nights</td></tr><tr><td>Timing</td><td>Cough worse at night and on lying down to sleep, not on lying flat while awake</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>Sister had a cold last week; recent convention travel</td></tr><tr><td>Modifying factors</td><td>Honey and tea help; no extra furosemide needed by the weight plan</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>Fatigue. Denies fever above 37.6 °C, chest pain, sputum, hemoptysis, orthopnea, ankle swelling, and weight gain</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

