  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute bronchitis, likely viral, after an upper respiratory infection (J20.9)</content>. She is afebrile with heart rate under 100, respiratory rate under 24, and a clear chest, so pneumonia is not suspected. A chest radiograph and antibiotics are not indicated. [S]<list><item>Supportive care: honey, warm fluids within her usual fluid guidance, saline nasal rinse, and rest.</item><item>Acetaminophen 650 mg up to every 6 hours as needed for sore throat, no more than 3 g a day.</item><item>Avoid ibuprofen and other NSAIDs and oral decongestants such as pseudoephedrine, which can worsen heart failure and blood pressure.</item><item>Repeat a home COVID-19 antigen test today and again in 48 hours. If it is positive, call the same day, because antiviral treatment would be offered at her age after a drug-interaction check.</item><item>The cough may last two to three weeks.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction, compensated (I50.32)</content>. Weight is at her dry weight, the jugular venous pressure is not raised, and there is no edema. Respiratory infection is a common trigger for decompensation. [S]<list><item>Continue empagliflozin and spironolactone.</item><item>Sick-day rule: pause empagliflozin on any day she cannot eat or drink normally, and restart when she can.</item><item>Continue daily weights and take as-needed furosemide by the written plan for a gain of more than 1 kg in a day or 2 kg in a week.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Hypertensive heart disease (I11.0)</content>. Blood pressure 128/74 mmHg, at goal. [S]<list><item>No change.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA Guideline for the Management of Heart Failure (2022); Appropriate Antibiotic Use for Acute Respiratory Tract Infection in Adults: Advice for High-Value Care From the American College of Physicians and the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (2016)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Nurse telephone check in three days. Call or return the same day for a temperature above 38 °C, breathlessness at rest or when lying flat, chest pain, or weight gain beyond the plan. Next scheduled visit 1905-09-28: six-month heart failure review. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
